  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"391\"><strong data-start=\"115\" data-end=\"130\">Bicarbonate</strong> (HCO₃⁻) in body fluids plays a crucial role in <strong data-start=\"178\" data-end=\"211\">maintaining acid-base balance</strong>. Measuring bicarbonate levels in fluids like <strong data-start=\"257\" data-end=\"268\">pleural</strong>, <strong data-start=\"270\" data-end=\"284\">peritoneal</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"318\">cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)</strong> helps assess the <strong data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"371\">metabolic and infectious status</strong> of the compartment.</p>\n<p data-start=\"393\" data-end=\"429\">This test is particularly useful in:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"431\" data-end=\"631\">\n<li data-start=\"431\" data-end=\"492\">\n<p data-start=\"433\" data-end=\"492\">Evaluating <strong data-start=\"444\" data-end=\"470\">acid-base disturbances</strong> in fluid compartments</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"563\">\n<p data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"563\">Differentiating between <strong data-start=\"519\" data-end=\"535\">transudative</strong> and <strong data-start=\"540\" data-end=\"563\">exudative effusions</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"564\" data-end=\"631\">\n<p data-start=\"566\" data-end=\"631\">Assessing for <strong data-start=\"580\" data-end=\"593\">infection</strong>, <strong data-start=\"595\" data-end=\"607\">ischemia</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"612\" data-end=\"631\">tissue necrosis</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong data-start=\"612\" data-end=\"631\">Elevated fluid bicarbonate levels may indicate:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Clinical Scenario</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Possible Explanation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Compensated metabolic alkalosis</strong></td>\n<td>Fluid reflects elevated systemic bicarbonate levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Iatrogenic introduction</strong></td>\n<td>Bicarbonate-rich solutions may increase fluid HCO₃⁻ (e.g., peritoneal dialysis)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Delayed sample processing</strong></td>\n<td>In vitro changes due to CO₂ absorption or cellular metabolism</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
